---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why isn't it notarized?",
    a: [
      "Apple notarization requires a paid Apple Developer ID, which this project does not have. Instead, Clipy Secure is signed with a long-lived certificate the project controls. That is enough to keep the Accessibility permission stable across updates, but it is not an Apple notarization and should not be mistaken for one.",
      "You can verify a download yourself: check the published sha256, and confirm the certificate leaf hash with <code>codesign -d -r-</code>. The install page walks through both.",
    ],
    link: { href: `${base}install/#verify`, text: "how to verify a download" },
  },
  {
    q: "Why no Apple Developer ID?",
    a: [
      "It is a personal project, and a Developer ID is a yearly paid membership aimed at commercial distribution. A self-signed certificate costs nothing, keeps the whole trust chain on your own machine, and — because macOS binds the Accessibility grant to the certificate's leaf hash — keeps that grant stable from one release to the next.",
    ],
  },
  {
    q: "Why isn't it sandboxed?",
    a: [
      "Auto-paste works by posting a synthetic ⌘V into whatever app is in front, through the Accessibility API. That cross-application control is exactly what App Sandbox exists to prevent — a sandboxed build never even appears in System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Accessibility, so auto-paste cannot work. This was verified on recent macOS from both <code>/tmp</code> and <code>/Applications</code>.",
      "So Clipy Secure's privacy comes from encryption, not from the sandbox: every clip is encrypted at rest, and the app has no network access to misuse in the first place.",
    ],
  },
  {
    q: "What is the Accessibility permission for?",
    a: [
      "One thing only: to paste for you. After writing a clip to the pasteboard, Clipy Secure posts a synthetic ⌘V keystroke, and macOS requires Accessibility permission to post keystrokes into other apps. It is not used to read other apps, watch your screen, or capture anything — and the app has no way to send it anywhere even if it did.",
    ],
  },
  {
    q: "Is there an automatic update check?",
    a: [
      "No. Clipy Secure makes no network connections at all — no update check, no telemetry, no sync. If you installed with Homebrew, run <code>brew upgrade</code> for new versions; otherwise download the latest release and replace the app.",
    ],
  },
  {
    q: "Where does my data live?",
    a: [
      "Your clipboard history is an encrypted SQLite database under <code>~/Library/Application Support/com.clipysecure.app</code>. Settings are in <code>~/Library/Preferences/com.clipysecure.app.plist</code>. The AES-GCM encryption key is kept in your login Keychain as an item named <strong>ClipySecure</strong> — so even the database file on disk is unreadable without it.",
      "Snippets are the one exception: they are stored in plain text by design, because they are content you curate and export yourself. Don't keep secrets in snippets.",
    ],
  },
  {
    q: "How do I wipe everything?",
    a: [
      "Inside the app, <strong>Clear All History</strong> (in the Privacy preferences, or the clip menu) empties history and memory. To remove the app and all of its data:",
    ],
    code: "brew uninstall --cask --zap clipy-secure",
    aAfter: [
      "That trashes the data and preferences folders above. The encryption key stays in your Keychain — delete the <strong>ClipySecure</strong> item in Keychain Access to remove it too. If you installed from a zip or from source, drag the app to the Trash and remove those two paths plus the Keychain item by hand.",
    ],
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="FAQ — Clipy Secure"
  description="Why Clipy Secure isn't notarized or sandboxed, what the Accessibility permission is for, where your data lives, and how to wipe it."
  structuredData={false}
>
  <section class="page-intro" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
    <h1 id="faq-title">Questions, answered honestly</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Clipy Secure makes some deliberate, unusual trade-offs. Here is the reasoning, without the marketing gloss.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="document-section" aria-labelledby="faq-list-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="faq-list-title">faq</h2>
      <hr />
    </div>
    {faqs.map((item) => (
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>{item.q}</h3>
        {item.a.map((paragraph) => (
          <p set:html={paragraph} />
        ))}
        {item.code && <pre class="command-block"><code>{item.code}</code></pre>}
        {item.aAfter?.map((paragraph) => (
          <p set:html={paragraph} />
        ))}
        {item.link && (
          <p class="section-link"><a href={item.link.href}>{item.link.text} <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></p>
        )}
      </div>
    ))}
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
